    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-13302",
      "problem": "Suppose $n=2pq - 4q + 5,$ where $p,q \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ Construct an argument that explains why $n$ is odd.",
      "worked_solution": "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a + 1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNow, note the following regarding the expression $2pq - 4q + 5 {:}$\n\n- All terms, apart from the constant term, have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The constant term can be written as $5 = 4+1.$\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{odd}}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =2pq-4q+5 \\\\  & =(2pq-4q+4)+1 \\\\  & =2({{pq-2q+2}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} pq - 2q + 2\\in \\mathbb Z }.$",
